The GLFW cursor position callback presents a challenge because it's a static callback function that doesn't inherently have access to your camera object. There are a few common approaches to handle this:

1. Using a global camera object (not recommended but simplest):
```cpp
Camera* globalCamera; // Global variable

static void cursor_position_callback(GLFWwindow* window, double xpos, double ypos) {
    globalCamera->updateOrientation(xpos, ypos);
}
```

2. Using glfwGetWindowUserPointer/glfwSetWindowUserPointer (recommended):
```cpp
// When setting up your window:
Camera* camera = new Camera();
glfwSetWindowUserPointer(window, camera);

// In the callback:
static void cursor_position_callback(GLFWwindow* window, double xpos, double ypos) {
    Camera* camera = static_cast<Camera*>(glfwGetWindowUserPointer(window));
    camera->updateOrientation(xpos, ypos);
}
```

3. Using a wrapper class:
```cpp
class Application {
private:
    Camera camera;
    GLFWwindow* window;

    static void cursor_position_callback(GLFWwindow* window, double xpos, double ypos) {
        Application* app = static_cast<Application*>(glfwGetWindowUserPointer(window));
        app->handleMouseMovement(xpos, ypos);
    }

public:
    void handleMouseMovement(double xpos, double ypos) {
        camera.updateOrientation(xpos, ypos);
    }
};
```

The second approach using glfwSetWindowUserPointer is generally considered the cleanest solution. The camera object cannot be directly fetched from the window object without having previously stored it using glfwSetWindowUserPointer.

